Author David Goodman joins fellow author and Napier University lecturer Nicholas Binge to discuss how to build online writers&#39; communities and run effective critique groups.<br />
This presentation includes guided exercises that will help you define what&#39;s important to you in your own critique groups and workshops.<br />
&nbsp;

NASA have just decided to formalise a Coordinated Lunar Time in order to make lunar astronauts&#39; lives a bit easier. When we eventually get to Mars, we&#39;re going to have to go beyond simply formalising a new clock system. We&#39;re also going to have to develop a new calendar to go with it. So what are the options? How does NASA deal with Martian time now, and how might our first Martian colony handle it?<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

From telepathy to flying cars, certain tropes used to dominate SF narratives but have since faded away. Have they evolved, or are they no longer relevant? Will they make a come-back, like wide leg jeans? Join our panel as they cast their mind back to those ideas so prevalent in past science fiction, discussing their impact and relevance for writers and readers today.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Sometimes the system (or society) is the bad guy. This panel will look at stories that engage with problematic systems and how they cause evil, as well as what heroes can do to fight them.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Co-writing is very popular in genre fiction. Authors have written via letters, email, and now in shared documents. What are the things to consider before, during, and after a co-writing project? And how do you ensure that your relationship will survive?<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

1. &quot;To take root among the stars&quot;: Survival and Adaptation in the Works of Octavia E. Butler - Riziki Millanzi<br />
2. Old Fears, New Understanding: Futurity and Evolution in Jeff Vandermeer&#39;s <em>Annihilation</em> - Isaac Sage<br />
&nbsp;

Lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) have been instrumental in shaping global heath through their use in COVID-19 mRNA vaccines. This presentation delves into the technology and prospects of LNPs, discussing their current use and envisioning their role in the future of healthcare.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Works of Grimdark are characterized by their dark tone, gritty stories and strong sense of realism in the characters and plots. Humour is a great tool in any writer&#39;s toolbox, and dark humour in particular can bring light and respite to even the darkest stories. But writers can&#39;t just add a Knock Knock joke and call it done. How do they use humour well and strike the balance of tone? How can humour be used to subvert reader expectations?<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Join your fellow convention members, and perhaps some big names, for a stroll (2 miles/3.2km) to and from Kelvingrove Park, north of the SEC, led by Farah Mendlesohn and Mike Scott. The route is wheelchair-accessible, and we will adjust our pace to match the abilities of the slowest walkers. We advise against joining this walk if you are on the programme at 10:00, because we might not be back in time for you to make your way to the Green Room. Sign up at the Registration Desk by 18:00 the previous day, and meet at 09:00 outside the Crowne Plaza hotel&rsquo;s main entrance.<br />
<br />
This walk to&nbsp; Kelvingrove Park goes over the railway footbridge which has a is a long but gentle slope in each direction.&nbsp;

Cannibal aliens in space. Skeleton monsters in the forest. Demons possessing city-dwellers. Horror can blend with any genre to create new stories. So is it more a mood than a genre? And does it matter? Our spine-tingling panel of terrifying talkers will discuss their favourite examples of science fiction and fantasy horror stories and what horror can add to speculative fiction as a mood.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Eerie, unsettling, and spooky tales are a perfect fit for audio dramas. Our panellists converse on modern horror audio dramas, like <em>The Lovecraft </em>Investigations, <em>The Magnus Archives</em>, and <em>Old Gods of Appalachia</em>, and how they use clever sound design, ambient audio, and other audio techniques to immerse their audiences in the world of their stories.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Meganne will recount the experiences that led her to be selected as a member of the European Space Agency astronaut reserve, from materials science studies in microgravity to a year working at Concordia Station in Antarctica, one of the most remote places on Earth, also known as &quot;White Mars&quot;. She will describe the tough astronaut selection process and discuss her hopes for the future, as well as the work she is currently doing at the UK Space Agency on the development of future space stations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

Roleplaying games can provide players the opportunity to embody a diverse range of characters with different backgrounds, gender identities, sexualities, abilities, and more. Our panellists will discuss the importance of diversity in tabletop RPGs, introduce audiences to a wide variety of decolonising and inclusive game systems, and look at how players can push beyond stereotypical representation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

As the great, late Sir Terry Pratchett once said, &quot;in ancient times cats were worshipped as gods; they have not forgotten this.&quot; No wonder we can&#39;t imagine our stories without them, no matter where or when they are set.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
From Garth Nix&#39;s Mogget to Terry Pratchett&#39;s Greebo and Maurice, Robert Heinlein&#39;s Pixel to Robin Hobb&#39;s Fennel, come along as our panel celebrates their favourite felines.&nbsp;

Have you ever been curious about what this whole string theory thing is all about? Have you been perplexed when your favourite science fiction authors mention &quot;Anti-de Sitter Spacetime&quot;? Then this is the talk for you. The talk will present a simple explanation of the foundations and applications of string theory and why it is so exciting. No prior mathematics or physics knowledge is required!<br />
<br />
&nbsp;

How well do you think you will survive the zombie apocalypse? Is it down to preparation, our ability to learn survival skills quickly, or our psychological make-up? The panel discusses the possible strategies required to survive a fictional apocalypse and how these might also prove useful in more real-world situations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;
